#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063980 :question/id #uuid "9c9b5972-f775-5cbe-8e66-2a0309f24f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-142" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\dfrac{15}{4}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061 :topic/math-academy-id 396 :topic/title "Calculating Slopes of Straight Lines"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e02620e-1019-5914-8098-ba774881b136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061406} {:answer/id #uuid "17564048-a958-5c0e-a338-b129c62f5059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061407} {:answer/id #uuid "2554c8c5-5780-50e4-ab6b-e554e598b67e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061408} {:answer/id #uuid "49ec5cf9-fb43-51f9-9959-bf5930e74d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061409} {:answer/id #uuid "59b2b87c-b66d-54c5-8c7b-